{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310875,"nonce":"pGBTClneguAIsrB3B5SxQW76tog=","title":"Testing Conventions","message":"- \"Validate outputs\" means comparing actual PRINTED output against expected output for each test file\n- Exit code 0 (no crash) is NOT sufficient — a test can exit cleanly but print wrong values (silent regression)\n- Must capture stdout from `rayzor run \u003cfile\u003e` and compare line-by-line against known-good expected output\n- Each test_*.hx file in compiler/tests/haxe/ should have its expected output documented or verified\n\n- Use `rayzor run \u003cfile.hx\u003e` (binary at target/debug/rayzor) to compile and execute .hx test files\n- rayzor run outputs a header line (\"Running ...\") and footer (\"Complete\") on stdout — strip these when comparing\n- Rust integration tests are in compiler/tests/*.rs but only cover a few test files\n- The bulk of .hx test validation is done by running them and checking printed output\n\n- When a test outputs wrong results, it MUST be fixed regardless of when the bug was introduced\n- Do NOT dismiss broken tests as \"pre-existing\" — if the compiler touches TAST/HIR/MIR/codegen, all tests are our responsibility\n- Every broken test after code changes must be investigated and fixed, no exceptions\n- Build with `cargo build` (NOT `cargo build -p compiler`) — the rayzor binary is in the workspace root\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `testing` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}